{"duration_api_ms":101650,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"d9eacd43-a74b-4488-971f-eaf7c69cb269","total_cost_usd":0.301403,"usage":{"input_tokens":20,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12956,"cache_read_input_tokens":236615,"output_tokens":9461,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":5811},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":12956},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":20,"outputTokens":9461,"cacheReadInputTokens":236615,"cacheCreationInputTokens":12956,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.301403,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":5811,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"safety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":10,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"`/work/answer.md` is final, and sol and I both marked it FINAL on the board. It has the condition, a proof, and a worked example where the two points differ.\n\n**Condition.** Let D = v_A + v_B + v_C − v_P and m = min(v_A, v_B, v_C). For positive values, the Vickrey-nearest point equals the centroid of F exactly when at least one of these holds:\n\n- v_P ≤ m,\n- D ≤ m, or\n- v_A = v_B = v_C.\n\nIf a winner's value is zero, F is a line segment and the two points always coincide.\n\n**Proof outline.**\n1. The smallest core revenue is R = v_P, and F = {0 ≤ p_i ≤ v_i, Σp_i = v_P}.\n2. Shift every point by the VCG payments (y = p − p^VCG). F becomes a triangle with some corners cut off, where each y_i is capped at u_i = min(v_i, D). The cut-off corners don't overlap, so the centroid has a closed form.\n3. The Vickrey-nearest point is now the point of F closest to the origin. It equals the centroid exactly when the centroid's three coordinates are equal.\n4. That happens iff u·(s − u)₊² is the same for all three caps u, where s = v_P − Σ p_i^VCG, the face's total payment above VCG. This holds iff no corner is cut (v_P ≤ m) or all caps are equal (D ≤ m, or all three values equal).\n\n**Example where they differ.** Take (v_P, v_A, v_B, v_C) = (3, 1, 2, 3). F is a parallelogram with centroid (½, 1, 3/2), but the Vickrey-nearest point is (1, 1, 1).\n\n**How it was checked.** sol wrote the proof and I went through it step by step and agreed. I also tested the condition on 3,000 random instances, comparing the exact centroid with the exact closest point: there were 0 mismatches. My first run reported 6 mismatches, but these came from an unreliable optimizer in my script; after switching to an exact method they disappeared. sol separately checked 6,400 integer cases with exact arithmetic and all matched. The answer file contains only the proof and does not mention these checks.","ttft_ms":2164,"type":"result","duration_ms":235110,"uuid":"5a689313-5b68-48f9-bccd-f6ed369c909d","ttft_stream_ms":1770,"time_to_request_ms":55,"first_content_frame_ms":1770,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
